The Editors
European Journal of Human Genetics

Dear Editors,

I am submitting **"Kidney representation and uncertainty in AlphaGenome
regulatory variant prediction"** for consideration as an Article.

Sequence-to-function models are increasingly offered as a route to interpreting
non-coding variants, and AlphaGenome is the most capable of them. The claim
implicit in a tissue-labelled output is that it carries information about that
tissue. This paper tests that claim directly, and finds it is not supported by
the evidence usually cited for it.

Three findings follow from one another. First, an audit of the model's 5,563
human output tracks against three independent developer sources shows kidney
occupies 1.85% of them, that all four kidney transcription-factor tracks assay
the same generic factor, CTCF, and that cell-type-resolved kidney accessibility
amounts to seven DNase tracks of which only one is from a documented adult donor.
Second, benchmarking 31,934 successfully scored fine-mapped eQTLs across all 49
GTEx tissues shows kidney cortex yields the fewest eligible variant-gene pairs of
any tissue, 59 against a median of 616, and remains the smallest at every one of
36 threshold settings. The tissue with the least representation inside the model
is therefore also the tissue about which the standard benchmark can say least.

The third finding is the one I believe is of general interest, and it is a
control rather than an observation. Re-scoring the same kidney variants using
every one of the model's 54 GTEx tissue outputs in turn, the kidney output ranks
sixth, no comparator shows a significantly higher correlation in paired
bootstrap over the same variant-gene pairs, and the outputs' prediction vectors
are largely shared. Two explanations fit equally well: these eQTLs were detected
in kidney but need not be kidney-specific, or the model makes limited use of
tissue identity. The design cannot separate them, and the paper says so rather
than choosing. The methodological consequence generalises well beyond kidney: a
benchmark built from eQTLs merely detected in a tissue cannot establish that a
tissue-labelled output is using tissue identity, because an eQTL significant in
one tissue and not another usually reflects unequal power rather than genuine
specificity.

I think this fits the journal's stated interests in statistical and computational
genetics and in bioinformatics, and that it will matter to readers on both sides
of the journal's readership: to those building or applying these models, because
the control is transferable and cheap to run, and to those working on kidney
genetics, because it bears directly on how much weight a tissue-labelled
prediction can carry.

The work reports no new experimental data. It is a secondary analysis of public
resources from ENCODE, GTEx and the EMBL-EBI eQTL Catalogue, together with model
predictions obtained under AlphaGenome's non-commercial research terms. The
benchmark set was constructed and frozen before any variant was scored. All
analysis code and the frozen benchmark are deposited, and every reported number
and figure is reproducible from thirteen numbered scripts, seven of which run
without model access.

Earlier drafts claimed that performance in kidney was adequate, that it was
equivalent to other tissues, and that missing experiments were the decisive
constraint. Each was tested and each was withdrawn. The paper is shorter and less
comfortable as a result, and I would rather submit it that way.

The material is original research. It has not been previously published, is not
under consideration elsewhere, and has not previously been submitted to another
journal, so there are no prior reviewer comments to disclose. I am the sole
author. I declare no competing interests. No funding was received. Ethical
approval was not required, as the study analyses only publicly available,
de-identified, summary-level data and generates no individual-level information.

Yours faithfully,

Christopher Lawrence
